I. Subject of Technology Contracts, Litigation Subjects and Contractual Liability
Any citizen, legal person or other organization that has the capacity to perform a contract may conclude a technology contract and become a subject of a technology contract.
1. A legal person of an organ, a public institution or a social organization may conclude a technology contract.
2. An enterprise legal person, a partnership-type joint venture that has been registered according to law and obtained a business license, or any other unincorporated economic organization may conclude a technology contract without being restricted by the approved and registered scope of business.
3. Where a technology contract is concluded by an internal functional organ of a legal person, under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed that the contract is concluded with the legal person to which such organ belongs as one party, and the legal person shall participate in the litigation and bear liability:
(1) The legal person authorizes such organ to conclude a technology contract externally in its own name;
(2) The articles of association of the legal person or relevant documents clearly state that such organ has the authority to directly negotiate and conclude technology contracts externally;
(3) The legal person ratifies or participates in the performance of a technology contract concluded externally by its functional organ.
4. Any of the following organizations established according to law, having a certain organizational structure and property but lacking the status of a legal person, may conclude a technology contract as a party and participate in litigation as a subject of such technology contract:
(1) A scientific and technological social organization that has been registered with the civil affairs department and obtained a social organization registration certificate;
(2) A Chinese-foreign cooperative joint venture or a wholly foreign-owned enterprise that has been registered according to law and obtained a business license of China;
(3) A branch established by a legal person that has obtained a business license;
(4) An individual economic organization, a partnership organization, a joint stock cooperative organization, or a private scientific and technological institution that has been registered according to law and obtained a business license;
(5) A privately-owned sole proprietorship enterprise, a privately-owned joint venture enterprise, or a partnership-type joint venture that has been registered according to law and obtained a business license;
(6) Any other organization that meets the provisions of laws and regulations.
5. A technology contract concluded in the name of a research and development project team relating to such project may be deemed a valid contract, and the relevant civil liability shall be handled in accordance with the following principles:
(1) If the project team is established by a legal person entity such as a scientific research institution, a university or college, or an enterprise, and the conclusion of the technology contract meets any of the circumstances provided in item 3, the legal person entity shall bear civil liability;
(2) If the project team is jointly established by two or more legal person entities, and the conclusion of the technology contract is authorized or ratified by the relevant legal persons, the legal person entities shall jointly bear civil liability;
(3) If the project team is formed by two or more scientific and technical personnel on their own, the members of the project team shall jointly bear civil liability.
6. Where a trade union or a technical association organization of an enterprise legal person concludes a technology contract, civil liability shall be borne in accordance with the following principles:
(1) If the property of the trade union or technical association organization belongs to or mainly belongs to the enterprise legal person to which it is subordinate, the enterprise legal person shall bear civil liability;
(2) If the property of the trade union or technical association organization belongs to or mainly belongs to its superior trade union or technical association organization, such superior organization shall bear civil liability;
(3) If the trade union or technical association organization has economic relations with both the enterprise legal person to which it is subordinate and its superior trade union or technical association organization, such enterprise legal person and its superior organization shall jointly bear civil liability.

VIII. Legal Protection of Non-Patented Technological Achievements
51. Non-patented technological achievements are protected by laws and regulations such as the General Principles of the Civil Law, the Anti-Unfair Competition Law, the Technology Contract Law, and its Implementing Regulations.
A non-patented technological achievement shall meet the following conditions:
(1) It contains a technical scheme or technical know-how comprising technical knowledge, experience, and information;
(2) It is in a secret state, i.e., cannot be directly obtained from public channels;
(3) It has practical value, i.e., can bring economic benefits or competitive advantage to the owner;
(4) The owner has taken appropriate confidentiality measures and has not provided it to others without an agreed confidentiality obligation.
52. A party to a technology contract who obtains the other party's non-patented technological achievements through the contractual relationship shall comply with the confidentiality obligations and scope of use agreed in the contract. The termination of the contract shall not affect the validity of the confidentiality agreement, unless the agreed matters have been made public.
53. The following acts constitute infringement of the right to use and transfer non-patented technological achievements:
(1) An employee, without authorization, discloses, provides, or transfers the non-patented technological achievements, stage-specific technological achievements, or design drawings, product formulas, or manufacturing process documents of the employing entity or former entity to others for implementation;
(2) Inducing others to steal or disclose a third party's non-patented technological achievements or product design drawings, product formulas, or manufacturing processes;
(3) Disclosing, using, or transferring the other party's non-patented technological achievements in violation of the agreement;
(4) Obtaining, using, or transferring others' non-patented technological achievements through other improper means.
54. Where, according to legal provisions or contract terms, the right to use and transfer a non-patented technological achievement belongs to one party, any contract concluded or act performed by the other party without the right holder's consent, which transfers or provides the non-patented technological achievement to others, shall be confirmed void, and the act of unauthorized transfer or provision of such non-patented technological achievement shall result in tort liability. A third party who in good faith acquires or obtains the non-patented technological achievement may continue to use it, but shall pay reasonable royalties to the right holder and bear confidentiality obligations. If the third party, knowing that the transfer or provision of the non-patented technological achievement is an infringement, still acquires and implements it, or if both parties collude maliciously to obtain and implement the non-patented technological achievement, they shall constitute joint infringement and shall jointly bear the obligations to cease the infringement, compensate for losses, eliminate the effects, and maintain confidentiality of the non-patented technology.
55. The right to use and transfer a non-patented technological achievement is legally binding on specific parties or the parties to a contract, and the right holder has the right to prevent third parties from obtaining or using it by improper means. However, if a third party independently develops or obtains the same or similar non-patented technological achievement through legitimate means, such as through lawful visits and inspections, reverse engineering by disassembling, measuring, and analyzing products manufactured and sold by the technology owner, and then using or transferring the technology, such acts shall not constitute infringement of the right to use and transfer the non-patented technological achievement.

IX. Technology Intermediary Contracts
56. When handling contract disputes arising from the breach of a technology contract by a party, the remuneration received by the intermediary shall not be regarded as a loss of either party.
57. When handling disputes involving a void technology contract, if the intermediary is not at fault for the invalidity of the technology contract, the validity of the relevant intermediary clauses or the technology intermediary contract shall not be affected, and the intermediary fees and remuneration collected by the intermediary in accordance with the contract shall not be returned.
58. In litigation of technology contract disputes, the litigation status of the intermediary shall be determined according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the intermediary merely plays a role in connecting and introducing the parties in the technology contract and is not at fault, it shall not be a party to the litigation of the technology contract dispute;
(2) If the intermediary colludes maliciously with one party to the technology contract to harm the interests of the other party, the parties involved in the malicious collusion shall be joint defendants and bear joint liability;
(3) If the intermediary conceals the true situation of one party to the technology contract and thereby causes losses to the other party, the intermediary shall be listed as a defendant and bear corresponding liability based on its fault;
(4) If a dispute arises due to the intermediary's breach of the confidentiality obligations stipulated in the technology intermediary contract, it may be heard together with the technology contract dispute;
(5) If the intermediary fails to perform the technology intermediary contract or other obligations stipulated in the intermediary clauses, the intermediary shall be listed as a litigation party according to the specific circumstances.
